\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Setup and Notation}
\label{sec:notation}

Let $A$ denote a true $K$-class categorical variable (e.g., sentiment with $K{=}3$ levels) and $A^*$ its observed, potentially misclassified version produced by an LLM annotator. The misclassification process is characterized by a $K{\times}K$ confusion matrix $C$ with entries $C_{jk} = P(A^*{=}j \mid A{=}k)$, where each column sums to one. Following \citet{hernan_cole2009}, we represent this measurement process using \emph{starred-node} DAGs: the true variable $A$ is latent, and the observed $A^*$ is connected to $A$ via a dashed measurement edge.

\begin{definition}[Non-differential misclassification]
\label{def:nondiff}
Misclassification of $A$ is non-differential with respect to outcome $Y$ and covariates $\mathbf{Z}$ if $P(A^*{=}j \mid A{=}k, Y, \mathbf{Z}) = P(A^*{=}j \mid A{=}k) = C_{jk}$ for all $j,k$.
\end{definition}

This holds when the LLM annotates from source documents without access to outcome information. Even without explicit $Y$ access, differential misclassification can arise if LLM pretraining data encodes $A$--$Y$ statistical associations---e.g., in political stance classification, strongly emotional texts correlating with the outcome may be misclassified at different rates, violating $P(A^*{\mid}A,Y) = P(A^*{\mid}A)$. Violations can also arise from outcome-conditional prompting, few-shot demonstrations encoding $A$--$Y$ associations, or retrieval-augmented pipelines surfacing $Y$-correlated documents. When violated, bias can deviate in either direction without the toward-null constraint. Practitioners can diagnose violations by testing $P(A^*{\mid}A,Y) \approx P(A^*{\mid}A)$ on a gold-labeled subset, though $Y$-conditional validation sets are rarely available in practice; our stress test (Appendix~\ref{sec:nondiff_stress}) covers $\varepsilon$ up to $0.50$ as a conservative upper bound, with violations below $\varepsilon{=}0.05$ causing $<$3~pp degradation. For $K{\geq}3$ exposure, even $\varepsilon{=}0.01$ produces sign reversal in 14 of 16 empirical configurations (Appendix~\ref{sec:realistic_epsilon}).

We study seven canonical DAG topologies (Figure~\ref{fig:dag_taxonomy})~\cite{pearl2009} in which $A$ occupies structurally distinct causal roles: confounder, exposure, mediator, collider, M-bias variable, front-door mediator, and instrument. Throughout, we derive probability limits under \emph{linear} structural equation models~\cite{carroll2006}, the standard framework for exact characterization of topology-dependent bias; robustness under logistic regression is verified in Appendix~\ref{sec:logistic_dgp} and~\ref{sec:fwl_nonlinear}.

\subsection{Multi-Class Bias Expressions}
\label{sec:bias_expressions}

For each topology, we derive the $\text{plim}$ (probability limit) of the naive estimator that substitutes $A^*$ for $A$. Let $\tau$ denote the target causal parameter---its precise definition is topology-specific (e.g., the coefficient on $X$ for confounding, the category-specific $\beta_j$ for exposure, or the direct effect $\tau_{\text{direct}}$ for mediation)---and $\hat{\tau}(A^*)$ the estimator using misclassified annotations.

\paragraph{Confounding ($A$ = confounder).}
When $A$ confounds the $X{\to}Y$ relationship, the OLS estimator controlling for $A^*$ has probability limit
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:confounding}
\text{plim}\;\hat{\tau}(A^*) = \bigl(\mathbb{E}[Z^{\prime}Z]\bigr)^{-1}\mathbb{E}[Z^{\prime}X]\,\boldsymbol{\beta},
\end{equation}
where $Z = [X, \text{dummies}(A^*)]$, $\boldsymbol{\beta}$ contains the true coefficients, and the target parameter $\tau$ is the coefficient on $X$. Misclassification produces residual confounding that monotonically approaches the omitted variable bias, but the sign of $\hat{\tau}$ is preserved: $\tau_{\text{sign\_flip}} = 0$ structurally.\footnote{This result is consistent with \citet{brenner1993}, who showed that the adjusted estimate with misclassified polytomous confounders can be biased toward or away from the null. Both findings hold simultaneously: misclassification degrades confounding control, producing residual bias whose magnitude can exceed the correctly adjusted estimate (amplification), but the treatment effect sign is never reversed. The distinction is between \emph{sign reversal} ($\text{sign}(\hat{\tau}) \neq \text{sign}(\tau)$, which we show never occurs) and \emph{amplification} ($|\hat{\tau}| > |\tau|$, which occurs universally in this topology).}

\paragraph{Exposure ($A$ = treatment variable).}
When $A$ is the exposure of interest, the biased coefficient for category $j$ becomes a weighted combination of true coefficients:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:exposure}
\text{plim}\;\hat{\beta}_j(A^*) = \sum_{k=1}^{K} w_{jk}\,\beta_k,
\end{equation}
where $w_{jk}$ depends on the confusion matrix $C$ and the marginal distribution of $A$. Unlike the binary case, where non-differential misclassification guarantees attenuation toward the null~\cite{brenner1993}, the $K{\geq}3$ case permits $w_{jk}$ to assign negative weight to categories with opposite-sign coefficients, producing \emph{sign reversal}. A near-permutation confusion matrix effectively swaps category labels, making sign reversal the dominant outcome (83.4\% under uniform Dirichlet prior). This failure mode is structurally impossible at $K{=}2$ because a $2{\times}2$ stochastic matrix with positive diagonal cannot reverse the sign of a single coefficient.

\paragraph{Numerical Verification 2} (Primary coefficient preservation).
Across 23 empirical LLM configurations, the primary coefficient (largest $|\beta_k|$) never reverses sign, while 61\% of exposure configurations reverse a secondary coefficient. Under 100{,}000 Dirichlet$(1,1,1)$ matrices with diagonal ${\geq}\,0.7$, primary sign-flip rate is 0\% across $|\beta_1|/|\beta_2|$ ratios 1--50; secondary rates increase from 0\% (ratio ${\leq}\,2$) to 44.9\% (ratio${=}50$). This reflects magnitude dominance: preservation depends on realistic accuracy levels, not algebraic structure (without a diagonal constraint, both coefficients flip ${\sim}50$\%).

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Large language models are rapidly replacing human annotators in computational social science, with researchers using LLM-generated labels for tasks ranging from sentiment classification to political stance detection~\cite{gilardi2023chatgpt, ziems2024can, egami_hinck_stewart_wei2023, baumann2025}. Crucially, most of these tasks are inherently multi-class: stance detection uses 3--4 categories, sentiment analysis 5, and disaster response taxonomies 10 or more. This shift promises dramatic cost reduction, but introduces a critical validity gap: annotation errors from imperfect classifiers propagate into downstream causal estimates in ways that depend on both the error structure and the causal design. \citet{baumann2025} demonstrate that across 37 annotation tasks, 31\% of hypotheses yield incorrect conclusions with state-of-the-art LLMs, rising to 50\% for smaller models; these errors are highly correlated across models (60\% agreement when both err)~\cite{kim_garg2025}, undermining ensemble-based mitigation. Analyzing the \emph{structure} of annotation error, rather than simply reducing its magnitude, is essential for valid downstream inference.

The transition at $K{\geq}3$ is not gradual but structural. For $K{=}2$, the stochastic constraint forces any confusion matrix with positive diagonal to preserve coefficient signs---an algebraic property of the $2{\times}2$ matrix dimension~\cite{brenner1993}. This guarantee \emph{vanishes} at $K{\geq}3$: the higher-dimensional confusion matrix admits negative effective weights on categories with opposite-sign coefficients, enabling sign reversal even under high accuracy~\cite{veirod_laake2001}. Under a uniform Dirichlet prior, 83.4\% of $K{=}3$ exposure confusion matrices produce sign reversal of at least one secondary coefficient; under realistic accuracy (diagonal $\geq 0.7$), 2.1\% still do. The primary treatment coefficient, however, is never reversed in any tested configuration---an empirical regularity we discuss in Section~\ref{sec:method}.

Standard classification metrics do not predict downstream causal risk: within a narrow $F_1$ range of 0.614--0.690 across three frontier LLMs, relative bias varies by an order of magnitude across topologies (from 4.1\% to 42.1\%), illustrating that \emph{topology}, not annotator quality, determines downstream risk. This fundamental asymmetry has received limited systematic treatment. \citet{dosemeci1990} and \citet{veirod_laake2001} noted the possibility of polytomous sign reversal; \citet{brenner1993} identified amplification for polytomous confounders; \citet{lee_wood_doughty2024} extended classifier error analysis to categorical variables and LLM classifiers, but covered only one DAG topology. We present the first unified analysis of $K$-class annotation error propagation across seven canonical DAG topologies, building on prior single-topology analyses~\cite{brenner1993, teblunthuis2024}. Our analytical predictions match Monte Carlo estimates within 1.6\% MAPE across 161 scenarios.

We address these gaps with three contributions:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Multi-class bias taxonomy.} We derive bias expressions for seven canonical DAG topologies (Figure~\ref{fig:dag_taxonomy}) under $K$-class non-differential misclassification in linear structural equation models, prove sign preservation for confounding and collider, verify it numerically for mediation, M-bias, and front-door, and show that exposure and IV can produce sign reversal. Binarization amplifies the gap: collapsing $K{=}3$ to binary introduces additional bias varying 154$\times$ across topologies.\footnote{Computed as the ratio of maximum to minimum binarization-induced bias across seven topologies under $K{=}3$ Dirichlet$(1,1,1)$ confusion matrices; see Appendix~\ref{app:binarization}.}

\item \textbf{Annotation Sensitivity Values.} As a corollary of the bias taxonomy, we define three complementary ASV diagnostics---magnitude, significance, and sign-flip---revealing a topology-dependent vulnerability spectrum from IV (maximally fragile, 52.6\% sign reversal) to M-bias (immune, ASV $= \infty$).

\item \textbf{Topology-aware correction guidance.} We evaluate five correction methods and prove a Frisch--Waugh--Lovell invariance result: matrix-based corrections achieve exactly 0\% bias reduction for four topologies where $A$ enters as a covariate---a negative result overlooked in the annotation correction literature. PPI++ achieves 47.0\% RMSE reduction but requires gold labels and is harmful for M-bias ($-$88\%).
\end{enumerate}

